  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component demonstrates strong foundational accessibility practices: native `<button>` elements, proper heading/landmark pairing, correct external link handling with visually hidden context, and visible focus indicators. However, it contains critical design gaps around the interactive map container's ARIA role, missing state communication for in-place updates, and weak programmatic association of the text alternative. These gaps will cause screen reader users to miss dynamic map changes and keyboard users to lose context when controls update content in place.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\nBefore reading the code, I predicted:\n1. The empty `<div className=\"map-canvas\">` would lack a proper ARIA role for an interactive map, breaking keyboard/AT expectations.\n2. In-place zoom/reset updates would lack `aria-live` announcements, leaving AT users unaware of state changes.\n3. Focus management after control clicks would be undefined or accidental.\n4. The text alternative link would be visually clear but programmatically unassociated with the map container.\n*Actual findings confirmed all predictions.*\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (blocks access)\n\n1. **Map canvas lacks interactive widget role and keyboard focus strategy**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/WatershedMapPanel.jsx:10` (`<div className=\"map-canvas\" />`) is an empty div with no ARIA role, tabindex, or focus management. The comment notes a third-party library mounts here, but this component provides no wrapper to establish the widget contract.\n   - **User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users, screen reader users\n   - **WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard), WAI-ARIA APG Map Widget pattern, WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)\n   - **Why this matters**: Interactive maps are complex widgets that require `role=\"application\"` and explicit keyboard focus management to prevent screen readers from treating them as inert content or trapping focus unpredictably. Without this, keyboard users cannot reliably interact with the map, and AT may ignore it entirely.\n   - **Fix**: Add `role=\"application\" tabIndex={0}` to the canvas div. Document that the third-party library must implement its own keyboard navigation model. If the map is purely decorative/static, add `aria-hidden=\"true\"` instead.\n\n2. **In-place map updates lack state communication to assistive technology**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/WatershedMapPanel.jsx:4-6` (`handleZoomIn`, `handleZoomOut`, `handleReset`) only log to console. Expected behavior states these \"update the map's zoom/pan state in place.\" No `aria-live` region, `aria-busy`, or state attribute is present.\n   - **User group impacted**: Screen reader users\n   - **WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages), WCAG 3.2.1 (On Focus)\n   - **Why this matters**: Users relying on screen readers will hear no confirmation that the zoom/reset action succeeded. Dynamic content updates must be announced programmatically, not just visually.\n   - **Fix**: Add a `<div aria-live=\"polite\" className=\"sr-only\">` inside the section. Update its text content (or use `aria-atomic`) when controls trigger: e.g., `\"Map zoomed in to level 12.\"` or `\"View reset to default.\"`\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (significantly degrades experience)\n\n3. **Text alternative link not programmatically associated with the map**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/WatershedMapPanel.jsx:14-16` (`<p className=\"map-alt-link\">...`) is a sibling paragraph, not linked to the canvas via ARIA or semantic grouping.\n   - **User group impacted**: Screen reader users\n   - **WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 1.1.1 (Non-text Content), WAI-ARIA APG Map pattern\n   - **Why this matters**: AT users will encounter the map div, then later encounter a link to a table without knowing it's the map's text alternative. Association via `aria-describedby` or `<figure>/<figcaption>` is required for clear programmatic relationship.\n   - **Fix**: Add `id=\"map-alt\"` to the link and `aria-describedby=\"map-alt\"` to the canvas div. Alternatively, wrap both in `<figure>` with the link as `<figcaption>`.\n\n4. **Focus behavior after control click is undefined**\n   - **Evidence**: Expected behavior states controls update the map \"in place.\" No focus management logic is implemented or documented.\n   - **User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users\n   - **WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 2.4.3 (Focus Order), WCAG 3.2.1 (On Focus)\n   - **Why this matters**: If focus remains on the clicked button, it's acceptable but must be intentional. If focus moves to the map canvas or a live region without documentation, keyboard users may lose context. In-place updates require explicit focus strategy.\n   - **Fix**: Document that focus remains on the trigger button after update. If moving focus is intended, implement `setTimeout(() => ref.current.focus(), 0)` and ensure target has `tabIndex={-1}` for programmatic focus.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists)\n\n5. **Touch target sizing may fall below recommended minimum**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/WatershedMapPanel.module.css:28-32` (`padding: 6px 14px; gap: 6px;`). Combined with icon size, the effective hit area may be <24x24 CSS pixels.\n   - **User group impacted**: Motor impairment users, touch device users\n   - **WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 2.5.8 (Target Size Minimum)\n   - **Fix**: Increase padding to `8px 16px` or ensure the icon + text area meets 24x24 CSS pixels minimum.\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (best practice not met)\n\n6. **Missing `aria-current` on active map state (if applicable)**\n   - If any control becomes \"active\" (e.g., a toggle-like reset), add `aria-pressed=\"true\"` or `aria-current=\"location\"` to indicate state. Currently, buttons are one-shot actions, so this is optional but noted for future extensibility.\n\n7. **External link could benefit from explicit icon + text pairing**\n   - `src/components/WatershedMapPanel.jsx:40-42` uses visually hidden text for \"opens in a new tab\". This is correct, but adding a standard external link icon (with `aria-hidden=\"true\"`) improves discoverability for low-vision users who rely on visual cues alongside AT.\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions)\n\n- **Map keyboard interaction contract**: The component delegates to a third-party library but provides no fallback or ARIA wrapper to guarantee keyboard operability. If the library doesn't support it, the map is inaccessible.\n- **Loading/busy state handling**: No `aria-busy=\"true\"` during async zoom/reset operations. Users may click repeatedly if feedback is delayed.\n- **Focus restoration after modal/drawer close (if applicable)**: Not in scope for this component, but worth noting if the panel lives inside a dialog.\n- **Color contrast on button text/border**: `#0b5fff` on `#fff` passes 4.5:1, but button borders use the same color. If hover/active states change background to white with blue border, ensure text remains \u22654.5:1.\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | Map canvas lacks role/live region; alt text unassociated |\n| Keyboard-only | MEDIUM | Controls work natively, but map keyboard support depends on third-party lib |\n| Low vision | LOW | Focus indicators meet 3:1+ threshold; text contrast passes |\n| Cognitive | LOW | Clear labels, explicit external link context, logical flow |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations/transitions present |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No audio elements |\n| Environmental contrast | LOW | Standard UI colors; no forced-colors overrides needed |\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n\nThe component passes foundational semantic HTML and native control checks, but fails on **interactive widget contract** and **dynamic state communication**. The empty map canvas div is the primary blocker: without `role=\"application\"` (or `aria-hidden` if static) and a live region for in-place updates, screen reader users will experience a silent, unannounced interface. Keyboard users face uncertainty around focus behavior after updates. These are design-level gaps that automated tests will miss but real AT users will encounter immediately. The component requires structural additions to the canvas wrapper and state announcement logic before shipping.\n\n---\n\n### Open Questions (unscored)\n\n1. Does the third-party map library natively support keyboard navigation and expose zoom/pan state via ARIA? If not, this component must provide a fallback or explicitly document the limitation.\n2. Are the zoom/reset controls expected to be disabled during async operations? If so, `aria-disabled=\"true\"` or native `disabled` is needed to prevent double-triggering.\n3. Is the map canvas intended to be interactive for all users, or is it purely visual with the table as the true data source? If visual-only, `aria-hidden=\"true\"` should replace the current empty div to prevent AT from announcing an inert region.\n\n**Mitigated by**: The native `<button>` elements, correct external link handling, and visible focus indicators provide a solid baseline. The fixes required are additive (ARIA roles, live region, association) rather than structural overhauls.",
